{
  "_comment": [
    "The corpus definition. `python -m src.fetch --dry-run` resolves this file",
    "into a concrete request list and prints the counts; it never touches the",
    "network beyond one metadata call per act. Read that output before you",
    "trust this file.",
    "",
    "SELECTION PHILOSOPHY",
    "Two different rules, because there are two different kinds of Act here.",
    "",
    "1. The recodified criminal/evidence codes (IPC, CrPC, Evidence Act and",
    "   their 2023 replacements) are selected by `mode: mapped`, using the",
    "   correspondence map the API publishes for the recodification. That is an",
    "   authoritative source, so the old/new pairs in this corpus are upstream's",
    "   judgement about which provisions are near-duplicates -- not mine. Taking",
    "   both sides of a map puts near-identical text in one index on purpose.",
    "",
    "2. Acts with no repealed counterpart are selected by `mode: headings`, on",
    "   the grounds that you cannot select by supersession what has no",
    "   superseded version.",
    "",
    "WHY A SUBSET. The full corpus upstream is 271,673 sections. Fetching all of",
    "it would take hours, commit tens of megabytes, and make every experiment",
    "slow. The target here is roughly 600-800 provisions, which is comfortably",
    "more distractors than a retriever needs to be honestly tested while keeping",
    "the snapshot at a few MB.",
    "",
    "LICENCE. Upstream is published under s.52(1)(q)(ii), Copyright Act 1957:",
    "the text of Acts, rules and orders is not copyrightable, but the editorial",
    "commentary layered on top of it is. This project therefore stores only",
    "`section.text`. `src/build.py` is responsible for not pulling in the HTML",
    "rendering, the judgments, or the note fields."
  ],
